<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Senegal</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Dakar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The westernmost country of mainland West Africa, independent from France in 1960.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a> joined the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in January 1959 to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a>, which became fully independent on 20 June 1960. On 20 August the federation broke up, and Senegal and the French Sudan each proclaimed independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a> was elected the first president in August 1960 and advocated a brand of African socialism.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Senghor retired in 1980 and in 1981 passed power to Abdou Diouf, who served four terms to 2000 and encouraged wider political participation. Senegal joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a> in the nominal Senegambia Confederation from 1 February 1982 until its dissolution in 1989. A separatist group in the southern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casamance">Casamance</a> has clashed with government forces since 1982. In 1999 Abdoulaye Wade defeated Diouf, the first transfer of power from one party to another. Macky Sall was elected in 2012 and re-elected in 2019.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Mass protests began on 3 March 2021 after the arrest of Ousmane Sonko, and Amnesty International counted 23 deaths in June 2023. Bassirou Diomaye Faye won the 2024 presidential election. In November 2024 he announced that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> would withdraw its troops and close its bases by the end of 2025, and French sites were handed over in July 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/senegal.jpg" alt="Senegal" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Original upload by Nightstallion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Senegal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dakar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Léopold Sédar Senghor, Abdou Diouf, Abdoulaye Wade, Macky Sall, Bassirou Diomaye Faye</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, with Wolof as the most widely spoken language</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Senegal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=senegal" aria-label="Senegal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4203 1632 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Senegal lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4523 1888L4550 1838"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4550 1838h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4523 1888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4523 1888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dakar. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=senegal">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
